## 1. Result

For the selected q79 Hartshorne-Serre orbit, let

\[
0\longrightarrow L_j\longrightarrow\kappa_j\longrightarrow E_j
\longrightarrow0,
\qquad L_j=\mathcal O_X(-H),
\]

have extension class

\[
f_j\in\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(E_j,L_j).
\]

The selected class is unique up to scale and is fixed by the stabilizing
clock

\[
Z_j=S^jZS^{-j}.
\]

For a reverse class

\[
\rho_j\in R_j:=\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_j,E_j),
\]

the independent diagonal Maurer-Cartan obstruction is

\[
\mu_{E,j}(\rho_j)=\rho_j[1]\circ f_j
\in\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(E_j,E_j). \tag{1}
\]

UST.G3L proves

\[
\ker\mu_{E,j}\subseteq K_j:=\ker q_j,
\qquad
K_j\simeq\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2. \tag{2}
\]

A deformation of the locked, clock-linearized object must be a
\(Z_j\)-equivariant morphism. Hence its reverse entry lies in
\(R_j^{Z_j}\), and (2) sharpens this to

\[
(\ker\mu_{E,j})^{Z_j}\subseteq K_j^{Z_j},
\qquad \dim K_j^{Z_j}=1. \tag{3}
\]

Therefore the selected physical branch does not require a five-column
search. Choose any nonzero generator \(e_j\) of the canonical quotient line
\(K_j^{Z_j}\). The immediate endpoint decision is the single exact column

\[
c_j:=\mu_{E,j}(e_j). \tag{4}
\]

There are exactly two outcomes:

1. \(c_j\ne0\): no clock-equivariant reverse Maurer-Cartan class exists in
   the locked Hartshorne-Serre carrier, so this route is retired.
2. \(c_j=0\): the entire line \(K_j^{Z_j}\) survives the local clock test;
   one must then construct the global shift map, alpha-twisted chain descent
   and the remaining physical endpoint certificates.

This theorem does not decide which outcome occurs.

## 2. The Connecting-Morphism Identity

The forward extension gives the distinguished triangle

\[
L_j\longrightarrow\kappa_j\longrightarrow E_j
\xrightarrow{f_j}L_j[1]. \tag{5}
\]

Applying \(R\!\operatorname{Hom}(-,E_j)\) gives the exact segment

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(\kappa_j,E_j)
\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_j,E_j)
\xrightarrow{\delta_{f_j}}
\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(E_j,E_j). \tag{6}
\]

By the definition of the boundary morphism,

\[
\delta_{f_j}(\rho_j)=\rho_j[1]\circ f_j=\mu_{E,j}(\rho_j). \tag{7}
\]

Consequently the open column has an equivalent lifting test:

\[
c_j=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
e_j\in\operatorname{im}\left[
\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(\kappa_j,E_j)
\longrightarrow R_j\right]. \tag{8}
\]

Thus one may close the test either by multiplying two explicit derived
classes or by constructing one explicit connecting lift. These are not two
independent assumptions.

## 3. The Canonical Fixed Quotient Line

The G3L elementary-transform sequence gives

\[
H^1\!\left(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2}\right)
\simeq2\mathbf1\oplus3\chi\oplus3\chi^2
\]

and the injective boundary

\[
H^0(HD_j^{-1})\simeq\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2
\longrightarrow
H^1\!\left(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2}\right). \tag{9}
\]

Taking \(Z_j\)-fixed parts is exact over characteristic zero. The fixed
source of (9) has dimension one and the fixed target has dimension two.
Therefore

\[
K_j^{Z_j}\simeq
\frac{
H^1(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2})^{Z_j}}
{\partial H^0(HD_j^{-1})^{Z_j}}
\]

is a canonical one-dimensional quotient. A Cech computation must choose a
basis, but the zero/nonzero decision in (4) is basis-independent.

The full five-dimensional \(K_j\) remains relevant for classifying
non-equivariant derived deformations. Those four additional directions are
not admissible entries of the already selected clock-linearized physical
object.

## 4. Orbit Transport

The q79 orbit atlas has

\[
\Gamma_j\xrightarrow{S}\Gamma_{j+1},
\qquad
Z_{j+1}=SZ_jS^{-1}. \tag{10}
\]

Hence the exact source autoequivalence maps fixed vectors to fixed vectors:

\[
S:K_j^{Z_j}\xrightarrow{\sim}K_{j+1}^{Z_{j+1}}. \tag{11}
\]

Yoneda products and connecting morphisms are natural under exact
autoequivalences. Therefore the three columns \(c_j\) form one shift orbit,
