## 5. Common Translation Does Not Supply the Line

The principal elliptic fiber action gives a tempting canonical candidate:
differentiate a common fiber translation of the entire triangle (5). This
candidate is identically zero in the required reverse group.

Indeed, let a simultaneous first-order deformation of the triangle be
represented by

\[
a_L:L_j\to L_j[1],\qquad
a_\kappa:\kappa_j\to\kappa_j[1],\qquad
a_E:E_j\to E_j[1].
\]

Writing \(i:L_j\to\kappa_j\) and \(p:\kappa_j\to E_j\), naturality gives

\[
a_\kappa i=i[1]a_L,
\qquad
a_Ep=p[1]a_\kappa. \tag{12}
\]

The apparent connecting lift is

\[
\lambda=p[1]a_\kappa\in\operatorname{Ext}^1(\kappa_j,E_j).
\]

Its image in the reverse group is

\[
\lambda i
=p[1]a_\kappa i
=p[1]i[1]a_L
=(pi)[1]a_L
=0. \tag{13}
\]

Thus a common translation, including the infinitesimal shared fiber-circle
motion, cannot generate the nonzero fixed quotient class \(e_j\). A viable
lift must be relative: it must change the extension/gluing data rather than
move \(L_j\), \(\kappa_j\), and \(E_j\) functorially together. This excludes
one natural but false shortcut without excluding a relative Koszul or normal
Cech lift.

